                            <a name="comment2825"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Waterboarded
</h4>

<p>
When the trigger is pulled on the Taser, there is no pain, really, just an involuntary locking up of every muscle in your body, from your jaw to your heels. It feels very much like holding onto a spark plug in a lawnmower with your left hand and pulling the starter rope really hard with your right hand (dumb teenage prank; don't ask). This muscle lock is followed by a tree-like toppling over that lasts for what seems like several moments, but is in fact over in a second or two, followed by (in my case) being caught by burly cops just before the inevitable head-thud into the ground. There were two small red dots on my skin for a couple of years, but no other lasting effects. In fact, I felt fine within a minute or two. I must also acknowledge that I was not actually shot with the Taser; instead, the leads were placed on my clothing, and then the trigger was pulled. This prevented the small barbs from puncturing my shirt. I blame this lack of authenticity on management, who enforced an unruly dress code there.
</p>
