                            <a name="comment2839"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Waterboarded
</h4>

<p>
The idea of a reporter undergoing water boarding and then writing about it, describing the full effect and allowing others to understand just how horrible it is, is an effective one. Something that people could learn from. To that effect, it's no different (in my opinion) from being tased for a story, really, except for the overall condition of the reporter afterward. Not that having a taser used on you and being tortured with the method we're talking about are the same, mind you, just that it's done so a journalist can relate it to their readers.<br />
<br />
Where I think it's really effective is that it could help people understand that this actually is torture. Unfortunately it's not as obvious as sticking bamboo chutes under someone's finger nails, or the various other methods one could perform, and unless someone puts it in the right words, people aren't really going to understand the feeling that the tortured go through.<br />
<br />
So how far is too far? Well, I think that a reporter volunteering to have bamboo shoots put under their fingernails would be too far. As far as I'm concerned, where dedicated journalists are concerned &#8212; as opposed to shock and awe journalists &#8212; it would be too far if you were needlessly putting yourself in danger and there was nothing to be gained.<br />
<br />
But of course, such definitions are generally far too subjective to place firm statements on and, therefore, only a journalist themselves can really gauge whether something is going "too far". (Or, short of that, their editor.)<br />
<br />
P.S. My word is 'tension'. That seems pretty fitting, as well.
</p>
